Stop leaf-flattening cc_library headers into ${EXT_BUILD_DEPS}/include/

`_get_headers()` in framework.bzl returns two lists from a CcInfo:
  * include_dirs — directories that are added as -I/-isystem to the
    compile and (via `symlink_contents_to_dir`) get staged with their
    directory structure preserved.
  * headers — individual header files NOT under any include_dir, which
    get staged via `symlink_to_dir` per file, which strips the path to
    the basename and creates `${EXT_BUILD_DEPS}/include/<basename>`.

The basename-flattening breaks cc_libraries that use `include_prefix`.
For example, `@capnp-cpp//src/kj:kj` exposes `kj/string.h` via
`include_prefix = "kj"`. Bazel creates a `_virtual_includes/kj/`
directory containing kj/string.h, but the actual on-disk path is
`external/capnp-cpp+/src/kj/string.h`. `_get_headers` checks whether
the on-disk path starts with any include_dir; the `_virtual_includes`
path is in the include_dirs list but the source path is NOT under it,
so `kj/string.h` is added to `headers`. It then gets symlinked at
`${EXT_BUILD_DEPS}/include/string.h`, shadowing libc's <string.h>
and breaking libstdc++'s <cstring> in any downstream compile that
walks `${EXT_BUILD_DEPS}/include` via -isystem.

This affects every BCR cc_library that uses `include_prefix` —
@capnp-cpp, @onetbb (tbb/time.h, tbb/semaphore.h), @zlib (zlib/gzguts.h
when packaged that way), and others.

The simplest fix: trust the include_dirs list. Modern cc_libraries
always expose their headers under some include directory (either via
`includes` or `include_prefix`'s virtual_includes path); both forms
end up in compilation_info.includes / .system_includes. Staging only
the directories preserves the `<kj/string.h>` vs libc `<string.h>`
distinction.

Risk: a cc_library that exposes a header file WITHOUT including its
parent directory in `includes` would no longer have that header
visible to the foreign_cc-driven compile. In practice modern Bazel
cc_libraries always set `includes` (or `include_prefix`); the
fallback to per-file staging existed for very old / unusual cc_library
authoring patterns. Worth upstreaming as opt-in (e.g., a feature
flag) but the immediate-need fix is to flip the default.

Upstream tracking: rules_foreign_cc issue #418 (open since 2020),
"Configure directory structure of $EXT_BUILD_DEPS".

--- a/foreign_cc/private/framework.bzl
+++ b/foreign_cc/private/framework.bzl
@@ -1029,16 +1029,16 @@ def _get_headers(compilation_info):
     # do not use quote includes, currently they do not contain
     # library-specific information
     include_dirs = collections.uniq(include_dirs)
-    headers = []
-    for header in compilation_info.headers.to_list():
-        path = header.path
-        included = False
-        for dir_ in include_dirs:
-            if path.startswith(dir_):
-                included = True
-                break
-        if not included:
-            headers.append(header)
+    # Skip per-file leaf-flattening: cc_libraries with `include_prefix`
+    # expose their headers via _virtual_includes paths whose source-
+    # path does not match any include_dir, so the original code added
+    # them to `headers` and `symlink_contents_to_dir` then placed each
+    # at `${EXT_BUILD_DEPS}/include/<basename>` — shadowing libc when
+    # any included file (e.g. kj/string.h) shares a basename with a
+    # libc header. Trust the include_dirs list instead; modern
+    # cc_libraries always expose their hdrs under some include
+    # directory via `includes`/`include_prefix`. Issue #418.
+    headers = []
     return struct(
         headers = headers,
         include_dirs = include_dirs,
